  

<h2 id="stop-reading-now-if-you-arent-interested-in-a-nuanced-take-on-both-of-them">Stop reading now if you aren’t interested in a nuanced take on both of them</h2>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_177bc256f0ee469c998e912e20ece048/_image_cache/35c742fa-2399-4aef-a36c-a789dc73b020/pasted-percent20image-percent2020260822194418.png" alt="Closeup photo of a reddish leaf with distinct woody veins. The word kindness appears in the lower right quadrant in white type written in a mixture of Bodoni Oldstyle and Comic Sans. Black streaks cross the text at various points in a negation of the veins on the leaf." width="1924" height="1440"></p>
<p><em>Photo and text by the author</em></p>
<hr>
<p>I’m not usually into the <em>would you rather</em> game, but <a href="https://pkm.social/@ellane/117090227148490589" target="_blank">this one</a> on Mastodon caught my attention. We were asked to choose which we preferred: an <span class="small-caps">AI</span> generated poster, or a poster made by hand but set in Comic Sans.</p>
<p>Unsurprisingly (given the type of people I follow) 98% of 677 respondents opted for Comic Sans! Some reported that it was a close call.</p>
<p>Other comments:</p>
<ul>
<li>I miss the days where Comic Sans was the problem<br>
</li>
<li>Give me a poor font choice made by a real human please<br>
</li>
<li>There’s no guarantee the AI-generated one <em>wouldn’t</em> be set in Comic Sans…or something even worse [Ed: scary thought!]</li>
</ul>
<p>For the record, I’m unapologetically okay with the careful, thought-provoking use of <span class="small-caps">AI</span>. Anne-Laure Le Cunff uses it to create illustrative artwork for her newsletter, and Mike Schmitz promotes it as a thinking partner. I use it on occasion for verifiable research and to pose questions to prompt my thinking processes. I never, however, use it to write anything or to create images for my work.</p>
<p>That 98%-against poll is telling us that a lot of people consider currently existing generative <span class="small-caps">AI</span> posters to be worse than the worst fast food. They both look edible at first glance, but almost all are over-seasoned to cover the blandness of their passionless souls.</p>
<p>Comic Sans might be the equivalent of Great Aunt Martha’s candied-bacon corn syrup marshmallows, but at least she made them with love — and we know what’s in them.</p>
<p>I was going to end here and bid you farewell until next week, but on re-reading I felt the need for</p>
<h3 id="some-balance-some-nuance-on-the-broader-discussion-around-ai.">some balance, some nuance on the broader discussion around <span class="small-caps">AI</span>.</h3>
<p>I don’t use it to write my posts, but I don’t condemn those who do when they approach it like <a href="https://elizabethtai.com/2026/08/19/life-as-a-techwriter-in-the-ai-age/" target="_blank">​Elizabeth Tai​</a> does.</p>
<p>Liz, a technical writer, <a href="https://hachyderm.io/@liztai/117137102823923883" target="_blank">put out a call</a> as I was editing this post seeking to connect with other writers on Mastodon who carefully, intentionally use <span class="small-caps">AI</span> in their writing workflows. Despite saying the equivalent of<span class="push-single"></span> <span class="pull-single">‘</span>If this doesn’t appeal to you, just walk on by’, one commenter went there anyway.</p>
<p>I get that the feelings are strong, polarising, even, on this topic.</p>
<p>As I replied to Liz —</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I might not be that kind of writer but I support your right to use <span class="small-caps">AI</span> in the ways that work for you. You have already proven you can write under your own steam.</p>
<p>I strongly prefer that people who have used <span class="small-caps">AI</span> assistance in their writing (beyond grammar fixes) state that up front, however. As you are doing.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Liz lives in Malaysia. She knows the Asian perspective on <span class="small-caps">AI</span> from the inside. Without an understanding of how people living in Asia experience the world, one can’t possibly understand — or judge — their choices. <a href="https://elizabethtai.com/2026/07/29/being-a-minority-on-social-media-sucks-sometimes/" target="_blank">Check out Liz’ blog</a> for a window into that world.</p>
<h3 id="dont-judge-me-because-i-sin-differently-to-you-we-can-all-be-better-humans">Don’t judge me because I sin differently to you (we can all be better humans)</h3>
<p>Whether it’s <span class="small-caps">AI</span> or fonts, we’ll make better, happier progress when we focus the bulk of our attention on progressing down our own path.</p>
<p>Be an activist if that’s what you feel called to, but focus your attacks on the system, not the sincere individuals who don’t share your concerns. We are, each of us, deeply flawed humans on our own journeys. The sum of your life has led to the choices (choices you are sure are right) that you are making today. This also applies to those who disagree with you.</p>
<p>I joked with my son this morning that when I’m gone, he should totally have part of the inscription on my headstone written in Comic Sans! It would be at the bottom and say something like<span class="push-double"></span> <span class="pull-double">“</span>She may have been a font snob, but at least she could take a joke”.</p>
<p>— <em>Not happening,</em> he said. <em>Do you know how much they’d charge for that??</em> (He knows, only too well: it’s around $7 <span class="small-caps">AUD</span> per letter.)</p>
<p>I still think it would be funny. Maybe I’ll put it in my will.</p>
